<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Democratic Party of Albania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Democratic Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> is a centre-right party founded in December 1990, the first opposition party allowed under the communist regime. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sali-berisha">Sali Berisha</a>, it won the 1992 elections, ending communist rule, and governed until the collapse of pyramid investment schemes in 1997, and again from 2005 to 2013. Its rivalry with the Socialist Party has dominated Albanian politics since.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/democratic-party-of-albania.jpg" alt="Democratic Party of Albania" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vectorized by Siar O · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_-_Partia_Demokratike_e_Shqip%C3%ABris%C3%AB_-_Rithemelimi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was founded on 12 December 1990 as an anti-communist movement. Avdyl Matoshi, Azem Hajdari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sali-berisha">Sali Berisha</a> and Gramoz Pashko took a major part in its founding. Most of its followers were dissidents who had been persecuted under the communist regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>. In the elections of 1991, the first since 1923 to allow more than one party, the ruling Labour Party had the greater advantage, including influence over the media, and the opposition could not reach the countryside. The Democratic Party promised membership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Community</a>, strong ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and a free-market economy. It came second.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The party won the parliamentary elections of 22 March 1992. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sali-berisha">Sali Berisha</a> became the second President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, an office that then held executive powers, and Aleksandër Meksi became prime minister. In 1997 the collapse of pyramid investment schemes led to civil unrest, especially in the south, and many investors blamed the government. The Socialist Party, the reformed successor of the Labour Party, won the elections of 29 June 1997. On 12 September 1998 Azem Hajdari was assassinated, and Berisha accused the Socialist government of the killing.</p><p>The party lost the elections of 24 June 2001 and alleged irregularities; observers reported irregularities too, though not how far they affected the result. In 2005 it won 56 of the 140 seats, and with its allies held 79, so that Berisha became prime minister. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 2009. The party and its alliance won the elections of 28 June 2009. After defeat in 2013 Berisha resigned as leader, and on 23 July 2013 Lulzim Basha won the first leadership vote of all members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party is regarded as centre-right, with conservative and conservative-liberal views, and it supports <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>'s accession to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. Its headquarters stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a>, about 50 metres from the parliament. After a third defeat in 2021 the party split between supporters of Basha and of Berisha. On 11 December 2021 Berisha's faction held a national council that voted to remove Basha, who did not recognise the result. In 2024 the Court of Appeal upheld a ruling that recognised the decisions of that council.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Democratic Party of Albania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1992 the <b>Democratic Party</b> of Sali Berisha won power, ending communist rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/albania">Albania</a></li></ul></section>
